 of time that I look at so be  secondly the early 1900s through the  1970s aviation really had kind of  transcendent significance and especially  because this was an era in which flying  was very expensive it was considered a  luxury and how to you know kind of  connotations of glamour and people were  fascinated by it so even though only a  small minority of people could actually  afford to fly discourse of and images of  aviation pervaded American culture in  advertisements in film in newspapers and  magazines and so my book really examines  kind of the cultural history of aviation  as a way of understanding how Americans  came to terms with and kind of  legitimized the United States expanding  global power so beyond the story about  aviation that's really a story about the  United States rise as a global power in  a particular kind of global power okay  and what led you to write the book so I  should say it was not a direct flight  whose a rather circuitous route so so as  you said in your introduction my primary  research interest concern culture  business and technology in the history  of u.s. foreign relations and so when I  was in graduate school I I had to write  a seminar paper for this course called  the American century which was taught by  my advisor John Christophe Agnew and  right before in the process of choosing  my seminar paper topic I happened to see  the movie catch me if you can and it's a  cultural historian I was really  fascinated by the kind of the  iconography of air travels depicted in  that movie and has been depicted in  subsequent TV shows such as Mad Men for  example and ABC's now cancelled series  Pan Am  so I started become to become very  interested in the history of Pan Am you  know first of all why did this one  airline have such an incredible cultural  alluring this period of time but then I  was also curious about why Pan Am was  called Pan Am because that name implies  some kind of connection to Pan  Americanism which in turn implies a  connection to the history of u.s. Latin  American relations which was also a  topic that I was interested in exploring  so I wrote this paper and I found out  that  the indeed Pan Am first expanded its  routes throughout Latin America in the  1920s and 1930s and had a very complex  and interesting relationship to u.s.  foreign policy in that region so from  there I started to think about the  relationship between aviation and u.s.  foreign policy more broadly and I  realized that even though there's a  massive popular literature on aviation  history at that point in time at least  aviation was really kind of marked  marginalized in historical scholarship  it was it was considered a subject that  was of interest to buffs not serious  academic historians so I really wrote  this book with the intention of changing  that with you know with the intention of  emphasizing that looking at aviation  really can help us develop new  perspectives on big questions in  international history and American  history so that leads me to the research  itself how how did you do the research  what was your methodology so there are  three main bodies of sources the first  was government documents primarily State  Department documents but I also did a  lot of research and presidential  libraries to basically understand the  kind of the foreign policy side of the  story the second group of sources were  cultural sources so advertisements films  novels newspapers and magazines from the  United States and other countries and  again this was a way of understanding  how people were talking about aviation  and it was like the fun part of the it  was definitely the fun part especially  working with the advertisements because  the imagery is just so rich now I was  lucky to have access to at Duke  University they have a very large  collection of advertisements and records  from the J Walter Thompson the  advertising agency which was one of the  most influential agencies in the US and  they did advertising and publicity for a  lot of major airlines so that was a  really wonderful archive to use and then  finally my third body of sources were  the records of Pan American World  Airways or Pan Am which is really kind  of the main protagonist of my book and  it's usually very difficult to get  access to corporate records but  fortunately after Pan Am went out of  business in the late 19th early 1990s  they donated their records to the  University of Miami so  was able to go and look at those and of  course Miami was was a very nice place  to do research especially in the winter  huh okay so let's talk about some  examples of how the commercial aviation  during this time period helped the  United States rise in terms of  globalization so - in two main ways  one aviation literally expanded and  strengthened the kind of the material  backbone of American power in the world  both militarily and economically the  United States worldwide infrastructure  of air routes and air bases facilitated  global commerce obviously it enabled new  and more powerful methods of warfare and  it it literally allowed the United  States to have a larger kind of global  footprint in many nations Pan Am  especially in the 1920s and 30s Pan Am's  sales offices served as kind of informal  embassies and then on the other hand  aviation as I said also really changed  the ways that Americans thought about  their nations role in the world and  especially because the kind of the  visual the visual iconography that  aviation inspired allowed people to  think about the world as one there's a  kind of unified global space so so  images of air routes for example allowed  people to kind of picture the world is  interconnected first of all and also the  world as kind of a single object of  influence for the United States so it  suggested that American power could go  everywhere it suggested that there were  no limits to that but and this is a one  of the crucial arguments of the book is  that even in kind of expressing this  kind of new expansionist imaginary the  discourse surrounding aviation basically  denied that the American expansion had  to had anything to do with traditional  forms of empire so the idea was that  whereas European empires were  territorial and involved conquering land  and ruling over populations the United  States would have an empire of the air  which would be based on  gets in access to commerce and in my  book I argue that that in many ways was  a fiction because airplanes obviously  need airports they need massive  resources and tracts of land in the  United States had had a very large  territorial footprint in the world but  still it was a different kind of empire  so it helped to aviation help to  reconcile Americans traditional  anti-imperialist sensibilities with  their nation's growing global power  so Pan Am was the first airline  basically so first in u.s. international  era yes so and they basically were flown  flying to South America Latin America  countries and is that because the  president was a latin american no so  here's the interesting thing about the  Pan Am that most people don't know so  most people when they think of Pan Am  they think about the president want rip  who is this kind of larger-than-life  very colorful character and despite his  his Hispanic first name he was a wasp  through and through he was actually grew  up he was the son of a banker grew up on  New York's Upper East Side he was named  after one of their families and an aunt  but only by marriage so he had no  connections to Latin America but so but  he also did not found PanAm and this is  this is what most people don't know the  airline was actually founded by a trio  of military officers in 1927 and they  were concerned about the security of the  Panama Canal because this was a time in  which a number of airlines in South  America had German and Austrian backing  and they were worried that these  airlines would could potentially  jeopardize the security of the Penn the  Panama Canal so they wanted to have a  u.s. airline in the region - first of  all protect the canal but then also to  uphold the Monroe Doctrine that the  United States is supposed to be the  dominant commercial power in the region  and and it's closed to further European  influence okay so in addition to the so  back in the 50s the first consumer  flight I'll call it a consumers like  we're average people although probably  upper-class average people we're taking  these flights so they were going  predominantly where in the world so I'll  back up and say  another reason why Panem started in  Latin America was that airplanes didn't  have the technology for transoceanic yes  exactly  so the earliest flights were actually  from Miami to Havana Cuba and it was  called the cocktail circuit because this  of course in the late 1920s was  prohibition and the people were flying  to to Cuba basically to party right but  but by the end of World War two of  course airplanes to develop the  technological capacity to fly worldwide  so by the mid 1950s especially with  things like the introduction of tourist  fairs which did make flying more  affordable Americans were really flying  everywhere I mean of course you know the  transatlantic market was was the most  competitive and most traveled market but  aviation did a lot to to basically  create kind of new new tourist  destinations especially in the  post-colonial and the developing world  and Panem in particular was instrumental  to that because in addition to in  addition to the airline the company  owned the Intercontinental Hotel chain  which kind of specialized in creating  kind of modern American style luxury  hotels you know in places like you know  places like Africa and you know places  that didn't already have that kind of  tourist infrastructure Wow  very interesting so what was enduring  the research what was something that  perhaps was most surprising to you that  you didn't expect  two things I mean I think certainly the  extent of the extent of military  airpower in the history of commercial  aviation I mean I knew obviously there's  connection especially on the level of  funding in the ways that technologies  develop but I certainly didn't expect to  find that Panem itself had been falen  founded by military officers with an  explicitly military purpose or one that  aligned with with Kimes military  strategy and so when I began the project  I very much thought that it was going to  be about commercial aviation because air  power has a very large literature of its  own but but I realized that the two the  two histories are really deeply  intertwined and then the other thing  which is a kind of funny I  I didn't expect that airline passengers  in the 1940s and 50s complained about  air travel just as much as we do today  for somewhat different reasons but  especially in the immediate post-war  period the nations kind of airport  infrastructure just could not handle the  volume of passengers at the time so you  know so things were happening like you  know planes were you know we're having  to circle airports you know for up to an  hour before they could land and you know  and there are all kinds of articles and  magazines like Harper's and time and  life year like why is flying so  miserable and it's just it's it's funny  because we know yeah because we now  remember that period kind of nostalgic  Lee is this golden age of air traveling  and in many ways it certainly was more  luxurious in terms of you know basic  things like you know you were served a  real meal and a martini and things like  that but the especially the physical  experience of flying was was not was not  as wonderful as we often think it it was  today interesting so Pan Am ended up  going under in the early 1990s do you  think it was because it was originally  founded on the military aspects of you  know air travel a VA ssin do you think  they you know missed the boat along the  way in terms of the commercialization of  air travel I think the real issue has to  do with the changing relationship  between the airline industry in the  state so basically until 1978 when  President Carter deregulated the  aviation industry Airlines depended  heavily upon government subsidies to  operate and so the kind of the so-called  legacy airlines the airlines like Pan Am  and TWA that had routes all over the  world they were never really financially  sustainable without those kinds of  subsidies so after the airline  deregulation Act they couldn't really  compete with these new low-cost carriers  many of which operated with non-union  labor and so I think I suppose that you  could make some arguments about kind of  the ways in which commercial aviation  started to have kind of less strategic  significance but I think it really has  to do more with with regulation and  deregulation and  those kinds of changes okay all right  and so what do you conclude in your book  so I conclude first of all that aviation  is a really important element of the  story of u.s. foreign relations that the  one that is that is often I think  unfortunately overlooked I agree you  know I when you when you think about how  important the air travel and bringing  people to places that they have never  been before I agree it's um I think it  has been missed  mm-hmm yeah I think that especially  because it was so culturally popular and  and it had this kind of you know rich  cultural iconography and discourses  surrounding and I think it's a really  the history of aviation is a really good  way to understand how Americans have  envisioned the world and that that's  essentially what the book is about it's  really it's a history of the American  global imaginary and the reason why I  wanted to examine this is that around  the time that that I began working on  the project a lot of historians were  pursuing transnational projects that you  know rather than looking at the history  